{"seconds": 115.1, "exit": 0, "final": "After Nxc6, Black plays bxc6 and White is only up a pawn but has traded off the active knight. Compare this to Nf7, which sacrifices the knight but wins material (h-pawn) with a crushing attack on the king.\n\nI'm confident **Nf7** is the best move. Let me write up the coaching note and main line.\n\nThe move is **Nf7**, a knight sacrifice that forces the Black king into the open. After Kxf7 (or Qxf7/Rxf7), White plays Qh5+ and after the king moves, Qxh7+ wins material and gives White a winning attack.\n\nMain line: 1.Nf7 Kxf7 (or 1...Qxf7 2.Qxf5 or 1...Rxf7 2.Qxf5) 2.Qh5+ Kg7 (or Kg8, or Ke7) 3.Qxh7+ (or Kf6 allowing Qf7+ etc.)\n\nThe motifs are: **sacrifice** (sacrificing the knight to open the king) and **attraction** (drawing the king forward).\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Nf7\", \"line\": [\"Nf7\", \"Kxf7\", \"Qh5+\", \"Kg7\", \"Qxh7+\", \"Kxh7\"], \"motifs\": [\"sacrifice\", \"attraction\"]}\n```\n\n**Coaching note (80 words max):**\n\nYou missed a knight sacrifice that wins material decisively. After Nf7, Black's king is forced to capture (Kxf7), and Qh5+ draws the king further into the open. After Kg7, Qxh7+ wins the h-pawn with a crushing attack\u2014Black's kingside collapses. The key pattern: when your opponent's king looks vulnerable and your pieces are attacking nearby, look for forcing sacrifices that pull the king forward and expose it to your remaining pieces.", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24", "move": "Nf7"}}, {"too